  "text": "**Solution 1 (answer 5)** is the most likely correct one, but I could not verify the literature here, so my confidence is moderate.\n\n- **Solution 2 (answer 3)** is probably wrong. It relies on a degree-4 positive result being out of reach, yet Braun and Oréfice-Okamoto's 2016 paper on degree-4 submersions points to a positive result beyond degree 3. Its cubic-case argument also has gaps I could not check.\n- **Solution 3 (answer 4)** cites Braun–Fernandes for a degree-5 counterexample. That contradicts Solution 1's claim of a positive degree-5 result from the same group, so at least one of the two attributions is wrong. Its degree-4 claim is plausible, but the stated sharpness is not.\n- **Solution 1** is internally consistent: a positive result at degree 5, and a sharp counterexample at degree 6. The degree-10 Pinchuk example it cites matches the commonly quoted degrees for Pinchuk's counterexample. The degree-6 counterexample and the Braun–Fernandes classification are the parts I would most want to check against the papers.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
